## Part one - the shape

Three declarative locations, each mirroring the **human** structure - primitives /
theme one / theme two - not the shape of the code.

```
                    primitives        theme one           theme two
ALLOWLIST           surface:'both'    surface:'composer'  surface:'console'
SCHEMA              primitives.*      prompt-composer/    ecommerce/
FIGMA MAP           (same three)
```

**The boundary rule:** the **allowlist is the authority**. The other two reference
it. The checker fails when they disagree.

### The hierarchy - where a finding belongs

An **index IS a pipeline IS a catalog**. They are one thing under three names.

```
user
  └─ their index  =  a RAG pipeline  =  a catalog        <- findings belong HERE
       └─ package            (prompt_sessions.user_id)
            └─ card          (one package = one agent-card-element)
                 └─ components   <- what the checker inspects
  └─ other indexes the user can reach
```

The checker already reports per index (`--catalog`), and every report carries
`catalog: <name>`. `/api/catalog/audit/{index}` already takes a name, so reaching
a second index needs no new endpoint.

**What is missing is only the association:** `users` has no index column, and
`DEFAULT_CATALOG = "prompt-composer"` is hardcoded in `routes/misc.py` - so every
user is served the same index, never their own.

### The recurring shape - mechanisms built, associations unwired

Four times now, the mechanism exists and the link was never made. None of these is
a missing design; each is a wiring gap. Look for this shape first.

| mechanism | built | association |
|---|---|---|
| per-index check + report | yes | works |
| named-index endpoint | yes | works |
| **user -> their index** | no column | hardcoded default |
| `get_allowed_tags(role)` | yes | every role invalid -> `basic` |
| `project_id` (package -> container) | yes | 0 of 261 linked |
| allowlist <-> schema | both | disagree (23 entries) |

